                <pb facs="0145" n="77" sameAs="frus:frus1867p1/pg77"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">[Public Resolution—No.</hi>
                        15.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>A RESOLUTION concerning the uniform of persons in the diplomatic service of
                    the United States.</head>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Resolved by the Senate and House of Representatives of
                        the United States of America in Congress assembled,</hi> That all persons in
                    the diplomatic service of the United States are prohibited from wearing any
                    uniform or official costume not previously authorized by Congress.</p>
                <p>Approved, <hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 27, 1867.</p>
                <pb facs="0146" n="78" xml:id="pg78"/>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sec.</hi> 34. <hi rendition="#italic">And be it
                        further enacted,</hi> That all officers who have served during the rebellion
                    as volunteers in the armies of the United States, and who have been, or may
                    hereafter be, honorably mustered out of the volunteer service, shall be entitled
                    to bear the official title, and, upon occasions of ceremony, to wear the uniform
                    of the highest grade they have held by brevet or other commissions in the
                    volunteer service. In case of officers of the regular army, the volunteer rank
                    shall be entered upon the official army register: <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Provided,</hi> That these privileges shall not entitle any officer to
                    command, pay, or emoluments.</p>
            </div>
